---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const results = 'https://github.com/rjpruitt16/aqueduct-benchmarks/blob/survival/envoy/RESULTS.md';

const footnotes = [
  {
    text: 'aqueduct-benchmarks: the harness, the raw data from every run, and the full results with their caveats',
    href: 'https://github.com/rjpruitt16/aqueduct-benchmarks',
  },
  {
    text: 'Chains: nothing is given up, everyone retries until it works',
    href: `${results}#chains-nothing-is-given-up-everyone-retries-until-it-works`,
  },
  {
    text: 'Wild: retries stacked across a chain of services',
    href: `${results}#wild-retries-stacked-across-a-chain-of-services`,
  },
  {
    text: 'Survival: capacity arrives late',
    href: `${results}#survival-capacity-arrives-late`,
  },
  {
    text: 'Wild, with Envoy plus what a team would build itself',
    href: `${results}#wild-with-envoy-plus-what-a-team-would-build-itself`,
  },
  {
    text: 'Aquifer, a self-hosted agent-native load balancer',
    href: 'https://github.com/rjpruitt16/aquifer',
  },
];
---
<Layout
  title="Retrying Forever Should Be Safe — Rahmi Pruitt"
  description="What five load tests of Envoy and Aquifer taught me: a refusal doesn't make work go away, callers retry until it's done, and a queue that recognizes a retry makes that safe."
>
  <article class="wrap post">
    <header class="post-header">
      <p class="mono-tag">// writing</p>
      <h1>Retrying Forever Should Be Safe</h1>
      <p class="post-dek">Five load tests of Envoy and Aquifer, and the lesson that kept coming back.</p>
      <p class="post-meta">October 2026 &middot; Rahmi Pruitt &middot; <a href="https://x.com/RahmiPruitt" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="post-meta-link">@RahmiPruitt</a></p>
    </header>

    <div class="post-body">
      <p>
        Over the last few days I've been benchmarking Aquifer, the agent-native load balancer I'm building,
        against Envoy. Five load patterns on Fly.io, from steady traffic to retries stacked across a
        chain of services to capacity that shows up late. The harness and the raw data from every run
        are public.<sup><a href="#fn1">1</a></sup>
      </p>

      <p>
        I went in to find out where Aquifer beats Envoy. I came out thinking that was the wrong
        question.
      </p>

      <h2>A refusal doesn't make the work go away</h2>

      <p>
        Envoy is very good at deciding where a request goes and at turning away what the backend can't
        take. But the work it turns away still needs doing. Its caller retries, and in practice callers
        retry until the work is done. Services, SDKs and agents don't give up after a fixed deadline.
        Some back off politely and some hammer, but they all come back.
      </p>

      <p>So I measured what happens when they do.</p>

      <p>
        <strong>When every caller retries until its work is done,</strong> Envoy needed 3.5 requests per
        job. Aquifer needed 1.0 and finished slightly more work.<sup><a href="#fn2">2</a></sup> Under Envoy,
        tenants that retried aggressively got 50% more work done than polite tenants with the same
        demand. Under Aquifer they got the same share and the same latency, whatever their retry style
        or region.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>When retries stack up across services,</strong> it gets worse. Four services, each
        retrying 3 times, can turn one client request into 27 calls at the bottom. Behind Envoy, the
        backend spent 55&ndash;58% of its executions redoing work it had already finished. Behind
        Aquifer, about 0.1%, because a resend carries the same idempotency key and joins the job already
        waiting. The same backend completed about 2.2 times as many requests.<sup><a href="#fn3">3</a></sup>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>When capacity arrives late,</strong> with traffic at three times capacity and new workers
        showing up minutes later, Aquifer ran every job at every delay. Envoy, even with limits sized
        for the capacity it would eventually get, ran 95%, 88% and 67% as the new capacity arrived 3, 5
        and 10 minutes into the overload.<sup><a href="#fn4">4</a></sup>
      </p>

      <p class="post-pullquote">
        Retries aren't the problem. A retry that the system can't recognize is.
      </p>

      <h2>I was calling Aquifer wrong</h2>

      <p>
        The first round of the late-capacity test went badly for Aquifer. With capacity 3 minutes away,
        Envoy answered 95% of callers in time and Aquifer 78%.
      </p>

      <p>Two things were wrong, and neither was the queue.</p>

      <p>
        First, Aquifer held a connection open for every waiting caller. Fly caps a process at 10,240
        open files, and at the peak it was holding far more. Once it ran out it couldn't accept
        connections, so callers couldn't reach results that were already waiting for them. Now it caps
        its connections, and tells callers who are far back in line when to come back instead of holding
        them.
      </p>

      <p>
        Second, the callers used exponential backoff with jitter. That's the right way to call a proxy,
        which forgets your request the moment it refuses it. It's the wrong way to call a queue, which
        keeps your job. A caller asleep in backoff can sleep right through
        a result that's already done. The backoff client missed 4&ndash;5% of results that finished in time.
        Clients that came back when Aquifer told them to missed none.
      </p>

      <p>
        With both fixed, Aquifer answered every caller in time when capacity came within 3 minutes.
      </p>

      <h2>What this makes safe</h2>

      <p>
        With an idempotency key, a retry stops being new work. It becomes a cheap question: is my job
        done yet? That changes what a reasonable client looks like. Instead of a backoff policy tuned to
        avoid making things worse, a caller can submit once and keep checking, every 30 seconds or when
        the server says to, for as long as it takes.
      </p>

      <p class="post-loop">
        Submit once with a key. Come back when told. Stop when the job is completed or failed.
      </p>

      <p>
        For agents that fan out, retry automatically and run for hours, that's the contract I want:
        unbounded retries that cost almost nothing.
      </p>

      <hr class="rule post-divider" />

      <h2>Where it doesn't help</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>You can build most of this yourself.</strong> Envoy plus idempotency keys in Redis, a fair
        queue and a dispatcher of about 500 lines matched Aquifer under stacked
        retries.<sup><a href="#fn5">5</a></sup> What Aquifer saves you is building and owning that.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>It needs a stable key and the backend's headers.</strong> Without either, it lost most of
        its advantage.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Big tenants wait longer.</strong> Aquifer gives every tenant an equal turn, so a tenant
        sending half the traffic doesn't get half the backend. That's deliberate: a long line shouldn't
        buy more capacity. A backend that wants to favor a customer can say so in the limits it
        advertises.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>A caller with a hard deadline is better off elsewhere.</strong> With capacity 10 minutes
        away, Envoy answered more of the callers that gave up after 5 minutes. Aquifer ran all the work,
        but only for callers that come back. Something that needs an answer in seconds belongs on a live
        connection to a backend with room to spare.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>The test has limits.</strong> The backend is synthetic, with a fixed service time and no
        database. The last round reran Aquifer with a fix and three client variants, and didn't rerun
        Envoy. An independent review recomputed the numbers from the raw data and they match, and its
        criticisms are in the write-up.
      </p>

      <h2>Envoy and Aquifer, not Envoy or Aquifer</h2>

      <p>
        Envoy routes and refuses. Aquifer remembers. Put Aquifer behind Envoy and Envoy keeps doing what
        it's best at, TLS, routing, auth and turning away abuse, while it can afford looser limits,
        because the work it lets through no longer has to fit the backend at that instant. Aquifer
        holds it, shares the backend fairly between tenants and paces it at the rate the backend says it
        can take.<sup><a href="#fn6">6</a></sup>
      </p>

      <p>
        I haven't measured the two running together yet, and that's the honest gap in this. Envoy's
        defaults are built for short requests, so the route to Aquifer needs longer stream timeouts and
        higher connection limits. I'd also like to see Envoy read the same headers Aquifer follows,
        maybe as a filter. Next up is Hatchet: the harness is built, and two reported Hatchet issues
        already reproduce on the version I pinned.
      </p>

      <p class="post-pullquote post-closer">
        Make retrying forever safe. Then let callers do it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class="post-footnotes">
      <hr class="rule" />
      <ol>
        {footnotes.map((f, i) => (
          <li id={`fn${i + 1}`}>
            {f.text}: <a href={f.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="inline-link">{f.href}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <a href="/content/" class="btn-ghost post-back">&larr; Back to Writing &amp; Talks</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .post { max-width: 800px; padding-top: 64px; padding-bottom: 100px; }
  .post-header { margin-bottom: 40px; }
  .post-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.2vw, 2.8rem);
    margin: 14px 0 16px;
  }
  .post-dek {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--paper-dim);
    margin: 0 0 14px;
  }
  .post-meta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--paper-faint);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .post-meta-link {
    color: var(--signal-bright);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--signal);
  }

  .inline-link { color: var(--signal-bright); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--signal); }

  .post-body h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 2.6vw, 1.7rem);
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin: 48px 0 18px;
  }
  .post-body p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin: 0 0 1.3em;
  }
  .post-body sup a {
    color: var(--signal-bright);
    font-size: 0.75em;
  }

  .post-loop {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--signal-bright);
    background: var(--ink-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 18px 22px;
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  .post-divider { max-width: 68ch; margin: 40px 0; }

  .post-pullquote {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--paper);
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 36px 0;
    padding-left: 20px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--signal);
  }
  .post-closer {
    margin-top: 48px;
    font-size: 1.6rem;
  }

  .post-footnotes { margin-top: 56px; }
  .post-footnotes ol {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--paper-faint);
    line-height: 1.8;
    padding-left: 20px;
    margin: 24px 0 36px;
  }
  .post-footnotes .inline-link {
    color: var(--signal-bright);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--signal);
    word-break: break-all;
  }
  .post-back { display: inline-block; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .post-pullquote { font-size: 1.2rem; padding-left: 14px; }
    .post-closer { font-size: 1.35rem; }
  }
</style>
